            "Every drug calculation begins by putting the prescription and the stock into the <strong>same unit</strong>. The metric ladder goes in thousands: <strong>1 kg = 1,000 g; 1 g = 1,000 mg; 1 mg = 1,000 micrograms; 1 microgram = 1,000 nanograms; 1 L = 1,000 mL</strong>. To go down the ladder (g → mg → micrograms) multiply by 1,000, moving the decimal point three places to the right; to go up, divide by 1,000, moving it three places to the left. So 0.05 mg of levothyroxine is 50 micrograms (0.05 × 1,000), and a prescription for 'digoxin 0.25 mg' with stock tablets of 62.5 micrograms means 250 ÷ 62.5 = <strong>4 tablets</strong> — but a nurse who reads 0.25 mg as 25 micrograms, or '.25 mg' as 25 mg, is a decimal point away from a tenfold or hundredfold error, which is why the rules exist: write <strong>micrograms</strong>, <strong>nanograms</strong> and <strong>units</strong> in full (never μg, mcg, ng or U); always a <strong>leading zero</strong> (0.5 mg); <strong>never a trailing zero</strong> (5 mg, not 5.0 mg); and query any prescription written otherwise.",
